The user invoked `{{command_prefix}}{{args_placeholder}}`. Treat
`{{args_placeholder}}` as the subcommand and its flags.

Available subcommands: {{subcommand_list}}. If it is empty or is not one of
these, say so, list them, and stop.

There are two kinds of subcommand, and they are not run the same way.

**CLI-backed** — `install`, `update`, `init`, `check`, `explain`. Run the
matching command with `{{scripts_path}}`, passing the flags through unchanged,
then do the work below for that subcommand. Read the command's full output —
findings are ordered by severity, not position, so `head`, `tail`, `grep` and
`jq` drop the ones that matter.

**Agent procedures** — `decide`, `spec`, `make`, `critique`. There is no binary. Do not try to run one:
the section below **is** the command. A CLI can check what these produce; it
cannot do their work, because the work is judgment and authorship.

## init

**Settle the surfaces before you run it.** Mode is the most consequential thing
`init` writes and the thing it is worst at choosing: with `--yes` it takes
`'/' → product` without reading the project at all, and `{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md`
rule 1 then makes that config outrank your own reading of the project from then
on. A default chosen in a second binds the project indefinitely, and density is
expensive to reverse.

You are the half that can fix this, because you are talking to someone who knows
the answer and the CLI is not. So, first:

1. **Ask what the product is**, in their words rather than Jig's — a marketing
   or content site, a signed-in application, an internal tool people use all
   day? A project usually has more than one of these, and each is a surface.
2. **Map each to a mode** using the table in `{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md`:
   `editorial` for first-visit content, `product` for the signed-in app,
   `operator` for dense daily tools. State the mapping in one line and let them
   correct it.
3. **Write `jig.config.json`** with those surfaces before running anything.
   `init` reads an existing config and honours it — writing one mode file per
   declared mode — so this is how a decision reaches the tokens.
4. **Then run the command.**

Where there is genuinely one surface, say so and move on; the point is that the
mode was chosen rather than defaulted into.

Afterwards, report what it detected, the brand colour it derived and where that
came from, and the surfaces it used — the output says whether they came from
`jig.config.json` or from the default.

If the token layer does not exist yet, this is the command that creates it. **Do
not author token values yourself.** `init` derives the brand colour, checks it
against the contrast floor in both light and dark, and records a checksum for
each file it writes; a file you write by hand is untracked, so `jig update` can
never refresh it again. `jig check` now reads the token layer back and holds it
to those floors either way, so a hand-written value will be caught — but caught
late, and unmaintainable, is worse than derived.

## check

The CLI decides the rules a machine can decide. **It is half the review**, and
its own attestation says `judgment=not-run` to make that explicit.

Do the other half yourself:

1. Load `rules.index.json` and filter it to the entries marked `pass: code`.
   **That list is the work.** Do not decide from the filenames which rules are
   likely to matter and read only those — the rule you would not have thought to
   open is the one you are about to break. Read whatever files those ids live in.
2. Return a verdict for **every id on the list**: `ok`, `finding`, or `n/a` with
   one line of reason. `n/a, no form on this page` is a verdict; silence is not.
   Leave the `pass: screen` rules alone: they need the rendered composition,
   which is not in any file you have, and guessing at them here is how this
   command and `critique` come to give one page two verdicts.
   `{{command_prefix}}critique` owns those.

   **This is self-review, and it is the weakest arm in the system.** You built
   these files; you know what you meant, and what you meant is invisible in the
   artifact. It runs here because it is better than nothing and it is cheap. It
   does not replace `{{command_prefix}}critique`, whose arm C reads the same
   rules against the same source with an agent that has never seen this
   conversation. When the two disagree, the one that did not build the page is
   the one to believe.
3. Merge both halves into **one** report keyed by rule id, ordered by severity —
   not two lists. A reader should not have to know which half found what.
4. Run the self-check `L-04` at the end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md`.

Then emit the attestation with both halves filled in:

```text
JIG_CHECK: version=<version> mode=<mode> mechanical=<pass|fail|skipped>:<n> judgment=<ran|skipped>:<n>
```

Take `mechanical=` from the CLI's own line. If `check` could not run, that is
`mechanical=skipped:0` — never `pass`, which would report a clean result for a
check that inspected nothing.

`judgment=`'s `<n>` is **the number of ids you returned a verdict for**, not the
number of findings. It exists because `ran` on its own cannot be checked: an
agent that read four rules and an agent that walked all of them both wrote
`ran`. A number that is far below the size of the `pass: code` list is a
sampled review, and it says so without anyone having to ask.

`judgment=ran` here means the `code` rules ran. It does not mean the page was
reviewed — nothing in this command looks at a rendered page, so a screen whose
stylesheet never loaded passes every part of it. Say so if the user is treating
a clean `check` as a finished review, and point them at
`{{command_prefix}}critique`.

## install

Report where the skill landed and at which scope. If it warned that a global
install already exists, do not work around it — that warning is the system
refusing to leave two contradictory skills for one harness.

## explain

`--layer` is the entry point for an agent that knows what it is trying to do but
not the rule number. `explain --layer` names the six and the question each
answers; `explain --layer layout` lists that one. The layers are a view, not a
location — nothing was renumbered to fit one, so `P-02 Button` sits in
`components` and `P-04 Form` in `patterns` while both keep the `P-` address they
have always had.

Print the CLI's output as it stands. It is already the rule's full text — do not
summarise it, and do not paraphrase the correction into your own words: the
wording is the rule.

**It takes a word as well as an id.** `explain contrast` searches every title
and body and lists what matches; one match prints in full. Use it whenever you
are about to reason about an area of the system rather than a specific finding
— before reviewing colour, before writing motion — instead of guessing which
rules apply or reading a whole rule file to find out. `explain <letter> --list`
prints one section, `explain --list` prints every id.

Reach for this before paraphrasing the rules from memory. A rule you half-recall
is the failure mode this command exists to prevent.

If the id is a `P-` or `M-` spec, the output says it is a specification rather
than a rule, with no ❌/✅ pair and no detector. That is correct, not a gap.

A well-formed id that does not exist errors, naming every id in that section —
offer the nearest one rather than guessing what the user meant. A word that
matches nothing errors differently, suggesting `--list`; that is a search with
no hits, not a typo, so widen the word rather than inventing an id.

## update

**Run this one as `{{update_path}} update`, not at the pinned version.** Every
other subcommand uses the pin so the CLI and these rules agree; `update` exists
to move that pin forward, so pinned it refreshes to the version already
installed and reports success for a no-op — "Updated Jig → <same version>" —
and nobody ever upgrades.

Report what moved and what was left alone. Files reported as skipped were edited
locally and are the user's; never re-apply Jig's version over them.

---

Everything in `{{rules_path}}/` is yours to read. Cite rules by id, and cite any
rule you deliberately break with the reason, in one line.

## spec

Plan the screen before building it. This command writes no markup and no CSS.

### Refuse rather than guess

Stop and say which of these is missing, rather than proceeding:

- `{{config_file}}` does not exist → run `init` first. Without it there is no
  mode and no token layer, so there is nothing for a spec to be specific to.
- The user named no surface → ask which screen. `{{command_prefix}}spec` with no
  argument is not a request to invent one.

### 1. Ask, two or three questions at a time

**This is a required interaction, not a suggestion.** Ask two or three questions,
then **wait**. Do not send a questionnaire. Do not write the spec and present it
for approval on your first response — a spec synthesised from a one-line prompt
and handed over for a yes is the failure this command exists to prevent, and it
looks exactly like the command working.

Ask about **structure**, not appearance. Colour, type scale and density were
settled at `init` and live in `{{config_file}}` and the token layer; re-deciding
them per screen creates a second source of truth for the values Jig exists to
centralise. If the user raises appearance, record it and move on.

- Round 1 — what is this screen for, who arrives at it, and what must they be
  able to do? What is the single most important thing on it?
- Round 2 — what content and states does it carry? Empty, loading, error,
  first-run, and the realistic range (0 items, 5, 500)?
- Round 3 — only what is still unresolved: entry points, where it leads, what
  must not happen here.

### 2. Run `L-01`

Read `L-01 · Layout method` in `{{rules_path}}/03-patterns.md` and run its five
steps against the answers. The spec's structural half **is** that output — group,
order by importance, space from the inside out, align to a grid, and state the
hierarchy plainly enough that the squint question can be answered against it.

### 3. Write it down

`.jig/specs/<surface-slug>.spec.md`. Frontmatter carries what can be compared to
a built page; prose carries the reasoning that no check can read.

```yaml
---
surface: login page
mode: product              # from {{config_file}} — state it, never leave it implied
confirmed: false           # only the user sets this to true; see below
regions:                   # in order
  - brand: wordmark only
  - form: the single primary task
  - recovery: secondary, below the action
hierarchy: [form, brand, recovery]        # most important first
grouping:                                 # which of L-01's four tools, per group
  form: proximity
  recovery: continuity
grid: { wide: 12, narrow: 4, container: --measure-form }
narrow:                    # must name a COMPOSITION change, not only a size change
  - recovery moves below the submit control
states: [default, loading, error, success]
deviations: []             # `make` writes here; `spec` leaves it empty
---
```

Every frontmatter field must be answerable by looking at the built page. If a
field cannot be, it is prose — put it below the frontmatter, where it belongs.
"Feels focused" is prose. "Three regions, form first" is a field.

Add fields the surface needs (`form: { steps, validation }` for a form, `nav:`
for a screen with navigation). Do not add fields nothing can check.

### 4. Get it confirmed

Show the spec and ask the user to confirm it. Then **stop**.

`confirmed: true` records that **the user said so in their own response**. You
may not set it on their behalf, and you may not treat your own summary,
`{{config_file}}`, or the absence of objection as confirmation. An unconfirmed
spec is not a weaker spec — it is not a spec, and `make` will refuse it.

If the user changes something, revise and ask again. The run is incomplete until
they confirm.

## make

Build the screen from its confirmed spec.

### Refuse rather than guess

- No `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → say so and run `{{command_prefix}}spec`
  first. Do not write a quick spec of your own to satisfy the check; a spec you
  wrote and accepted in the same breath is the thing the confirmation exists to
  prevent.
- `confirmed: false` → the spec exists but nobody has agreed to it. Ask for
  confirmation and stop.

### Build

Follow the spec's structure. Then the ordinary rules apply: tokens by semantic
name, the relevant `03-patterns.md` section for each component, `05-copy.md` for
every string.

### Record what you changed

Building reveals that a spec was wrong somewhere — that is normal and expected.
**Silence is the failure, not the change.** Append to `deviations:`:

```yaml
deviations:
  - field: form.steps
    from: 1
    to: 2
    why: "password reset needs an email round-trip; one screen cannot hold both"
```

A spec that quietly stops describing the page is worse than no spec: a later
review then validates the page against fiction and reports it clean.

### Finish

Run `check`, then the self-check at the end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md`,
and say which fields of the spec the built page satisfies and which it does not.

## critique

Review a built screen. This is **not** a second `check`, and the difference is
not effort — it is evidence.

`check` reads files. `critique` looks at the page **and** reads the source
against the rules `check` has no detector for. A hierarchy failure is in no
file: it is in what the files produce together, so no amount of careful reading
finds it. That is what the render arm is for.

Every judgment rule says which pass owns it, in `pass:`. The corpus divides
three ways, and each part needs a different instrument:

| | rules | instrument |
|---|---|---|
| mechanical, has a `detector` | a small minority | `check`, deterministic |
| judgment, `pass: screen` | the render | arm A, looking at the page |
| judgment, `pass: code` | **the largest group** | arm C, reading the source |

The third one is the reason this command exists in its current form. `check`
judges only what a detector can decide, which is a fraction of the corpus. Every
other `pass: code` rule is judgment, and until something reads the source against
it, it is enforced by nothing but the building agent having remembered it. An
agent under load does not remember 60-odd rules; it consults the handful it
thought to search for, and ships the rest unchecked.

No two arms judge the same rule, which is why they can never hand a user
competing verdicts on one id.

### Refuse rather than guess

- The user named no surface → ask which screen.
- There is no `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → say so and continue **rules-only**,
  reporting `spec=missing`. Do not invent the spec the page should have had; a
  spec written after the page, from the page, agrees with it by construction.

### 1. Three assessments that cannot see each other

Run all three. **No arm may see another's output**, and none may see the
conversation that built the page. Running them in one head anchors them to each
other; do not shortcut it for cost, speed or context size.

- **A — the reader.** Delegate to a subagent. Give it the URL or file, the spec,
  and the corpus. It judges the `pass: screen` rules against the render. Do
  **not** give it the build conversation. If it needs that conversation, it is
  not reviewing the page, it is agreeing with itself.
- **B — the machine.** Run `check`. Deterministic, same input same output. It
  judges the rules that carry a `detector`, and nothing else — its own
  attestation says `judgment=not-run`, and that is a true statement about the
  rest of the corpus, not a formality.
- **C — the source reader.** A second subagent, separate from A. Give it the
  source files, the spec, and the corpus, and no render. It judges every
  `pass: code` judgment rule — the group `check` cannot reach.

Each reader is a subagent for the same reason a writer does not proofread their
own copy. An agent that built the page knows what it meant, and what it meant is
invisible in the artifact — which is the one thing a reviewer must not have.

A and C are kept apart for a narrower reason: an arm that has seen the render
stops reading the source and starts confirming the picture. They answer to
different evidence and must not share it.

### 1a. Arms A and C walk the index. They do not search it.

**This is not a style preference. It is the difference between covering the
corpus and sampling it.**

Load `rules.index.json`, filter to the pass the arm owns, and return a verdict
for **every id in that list**: `ok`, `finding`, or `n/a` with one line of
reason. A rule that does not apply is still answered — `n/a, this page has no
form` is a verdict.

Searching the corpus is how a *builder* works, and it is the right method there:
it finds the rules you can already name. It cannot find the rule for the mistake
you do not know you are making, which is the only kind of rule worth writing
down. A pass driven by search returns what the agent thought to look for, and
reports nothing about everything else — indistinguishable, in the output, from a
clean page.

The builder cannot do this. It is holding a brief, a spec and a half-written
page, and it will drop most of the corpus under that load. An arm with one job
can walk all of it, which is the whole reason the work is split.

**Never narrow the list by guessing relevance before reading.** Filter by `pass`,
and by mode where a rule is mode-gated. Nothing else. An arm that decides in
advance which rules are worth checking has reintroduced search with extra
steps.

### 2. Look at the page

If a browser is available this is **required**, not optional:

- Render at a narrow width (~400px) and a wide one (~1280px).
- Compare them. Did narrow width change the **composition**, or only the
  **dimensions**? Reordering, stacking, collapsing, a different control — that is
  composition. The same layout with smaller numbers is not.
- Check the page is styled at all. A stylesheet that 404s renders a page that
  passes every file-based check ever written.
- Run the squint test from `L-01` against the render, not the analogue. The
  analogue exists for when there is no browser.

If no browser is available, say so in one line and use `L-01`'s analogue. Never
skip it silently, and never report a render you did not do.

### 3. Compare the page to its spec

For each frontmatter field, say whether the built page satisfies it. A difference
is a finding **unless** `deviations:` already explains it — that is what the
recorded deviation is for. A difference explained by neither is the more serious
finding, because it means the spec has quietly stopped describing the page.

### 4. Report

Merge both assessments into one report, keyed by rule id, ordered by severity.
Say explicitly where the two agree, what only the machine found, what only the
reader found, and which machine findings are false positives. **The disagreement
is information, not noise to smooth over.**

Say what is right as well as what is wrong — two or three things, and why they
work. A review that only lists faults tells the reader nothing about what to
preserve while fixing them.

Then the attestation:

```text
JIG_CRITIQUE: version=<version> mode=<mode> surface=<name> spec=<confirmed|missing|unconfirmed> rendered=<yes|no> screen=<ran|skipped>:<n> code=<ran|skipped>:<n> mechanical=<pass|fail|skipped>:<n>
```

Three counts because there are three arms, and each can fail independently.
`mechanical=` is `check`'s result and uses `check`'s own field name deliberately,
so the two records line up. `screen=` and `code=` are the two reader arms, and
their `<n>` is **the number of rules the arm returned a verdict for** — not the
number of findings. An arm that walked the index reports the size of the list it
walked; an arm reporting far fewer did not walk it, and the number is where that
shows.

`code=skipped:0` is the state this command was rebuilt to make visible. It used
to be unsayable: the largest group of rules in the corpus went unjudged and the
report looked complete.

`rendered=no` is not a detail. Without it a clean report reads as "this page is
good" when it can only mean "the files are good" — and a page whose stylesheet
never loaded satisfies every file-based check there is.

## decide

Write `DECISIONS.md` beside the token layer — `jig/DECISIONS.md` by default, or
wherever `brand` in `{{config_file}}` puts the token files.

Every other command is blocked until this exists, so it is worth being exact
about what it is for.

### What belongs in it, and what does not

The token layer already holds **values**, and `{{config_file}}` already holds
**mode**. Neither can hold a **reason**. This file is the reasons.

- ✅ *"Our accent arrives as a whole field or not at all — a full-bleed section,
  a solid button, a filled state. Never a 2px underline, never an icon tint.
  Its authority comes from arriving in quantity, rarely."*
- ❌ *"The accent is `#FF6347`."* — that is a token. Restating it here creates
  two places to change it, and they will disagree.
- ❌ *"Use 4.5:1 contrast."* — that is `C-19`, and it is true of every project.
  A universal rule copied into a project file is a rule that can rot locally.

The test: **could this be derived from the tokens, the mode, or a numbered rule?**
If yes, it is already written down somewhere better. If no, it is a decision, and
this is where it lives.

### How to write it

**Interview, do not infer.** Two or three questions per round, then wait. What
you are looking for is the places where this project has already chosen
something, or will have to:

- Round 1 — what is this product, and who is it for in a sentence the team would
  recognise? What should it never look like? Name real products, not adjectives.
- Round 2 — for each thing the tokens already set, is there a rule about *how* it
  is used? Where does the brand colour go, and where is it forbidden? What earns
  emphasis?
- Round 3 — what has the team already argued about, or reversed? A decision with
  a history is the one most worth recording, because it is the one most likely to
  be re-made wrongly.

**Write each decision as a named rule with its reason attached**, in the project's
own vocabulary:

```markdown
### The Stamp Rule

The accent appears as a whole field or not at all. A full-bleed section, a solid
button, a filled active state — never a 2px underline, never a small icon tint,
never a gradient stop. Its authority comes from arriving in quantity, rarely.

**Why:** thinly spread, it reads as decoration and stops meaning anything. The
audit test: if a coloured element is smaller than a section band, it is probably
wrong.
```

A name gives the team something to cite in review. A reason lets a future agent
tell when the rule does not apply, which a bare instruction never can.

### Finish

Show it and ask the user to confirm it is right before you stop. Leave no
`[TODO]` markers — the gate treats them as an unwritten file, correctly, because
a placeholder decision is not a decision.
